WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.640
Marvel fans, you can all breathe a sigh of
relief the cinematic universe you know and

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.960
love isn't about to get any more complicated
than it already is.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.990
Before he made his MCU debut as the eyepatch-wearing
S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:16.199
badass Nick Fury, Samuel L. Jackson was part
of another huge movie franchise: Star Wars.

00:00:16.199 --> 00:00:20.939
There, he played Mace Windu, the revered Jedi
Master who wielded an purple-bladed lightsaber

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:24.650
and sat on the Jedi High Council in the final
years of the Galactic Republic.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.280
"You refer to the prophecy of the one who
will bring balance to the Force.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.180
You believe it's this… boy?"

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.899
Fans love that Jackson is part of two universally-loved
film series, and they nearly flipped their

00:00:35.899 --> 00:00:38.510
lids when the MCU finally made a wink at that
fact.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:42.949
If you'll recall, Jackson's Nick Fury mentioned
Star Wars in Spider-Man: Far From Home, when

00:00:42.949 --> 00:00:46.460
he told Tom Holland's Peter Parker that he
wouldn't understand the Shakespeare quote

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:50.960
"uneasy lies the head that wears a crown"
because "it wasn't a Star Wars reference."

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.640
That led many to question why Peter a confirmed
Star Wars enthusiast, as established by the

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:00.410
events of Captain America: Civil War and Spider-Man:
Homecoming didn't comment that Fury and Mace

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:03.390
Windu look exactly alike when he officially
met the S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:04.660
agent in Far From Home.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.520
It also caused some Marvel fans to theorize
that perhaps Fury and Windu are the same person

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.200
or, at the very least, that Fury may have
been leading a double life as an actor.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.740
“OH COME ON!”

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.440
Now, we have an official answer to the question.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.100
Avengers: Endgame directors Joe and Anthony
Russo confirmed during an appearance on Lucasfilm's

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:26.680
YouTube web series The Star Wars Show that
Fury and Windu aren't the same person, Fury

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.160
isn't the actor who portrayed Windu in the
Star Wars franchise, and there's no MCU plot

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.190
hole to patch up now that Fury made a Star
Wars reference.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:36.600
Sorry theorists this Marvel myth has been
busted!

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:38.860
"You… have lost."

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:40.260
"No…

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.120
NO!

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:43.080
NO!!"

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.300
Anthony explained that all Marvel actors who
have also appeared in the Star Wars world,

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:47.800
well…

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.840
"They just look like those characters."

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:51.600
Well, yeah… we figured.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.640
But the tongue-in-cheek speculation sure was
fun while it lasted.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:58.800
The Russo Brothers' explanation actually clears
up several other mysteries of this particular

00:01:58.800 --> 00:01:59.300
sort.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.520
If Fury just looks like the guy who played
Windu, then one can assume that Paul Bettany's

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.170
Vision simply bears a striking resemblance
to the person who played Dryden Vos, the leader

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:12.019
of the crime syndicate Crimson Dawn as seen
in Solo: A Star Wars Story.

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:16.319
The same goes for Donald Glover: he played
Aaron Davis in Spider-Man: Homecoming and

00:02:16.319 --> 00:02:21.099
young Lando Calrissian in Solo so, within
the new rules of the MCU, Davis only looks

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.580
like the actor behind Calrissian.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:27.099
Heck, this rule can be applied to every Star
Wars actor who has also appeared in a Marvel

00:02:27.099 --> 00:02:31.430
movie: Natalie Portman, who was Queen Padmé
Amidala in the Star Wars prequels and will

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:36.269
reprise her Thor franchise role as Jane Foster
in Thor: Love and Thunder; Ben Mendelsohn,

00:02:36.269 --> 00:02:41.489
who was Director Orson Krennic in Rogue One:
A Star Wars Story and Talos in Captain Marvel

00:02:41.489 --> 00:02:46.249
and Spider-Man: Far From Home; and Felicity
Jones, who portrayed Jyn Erso in Rogue One

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:50.880
and unassuming assistant to Harry Osborn,
Felicia Hardy, in The Amazing Spider-Man 2.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:55.170
Thanks to Joe and Anthony Russo's explanation,
all these stars don't damage the fabric of

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:58.910
the MCU or the Star Wars franchise by appearing
in both film series.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:02.260
Though it's less riveting than the notion
that each pair of characters are the same

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:07.030
person and that there's some wild Lucasfilm-Marvel
Studios inter-connectivity on the horizon,

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:10.000
it keeps the MCU from becoming more difficult
to understand.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.780
After all, we're already nearly two dozen
flicks in!

